  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 116 PM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSurface high pressure has built into the region and a cool and a dry \nair mass is in place. Temperatures have risen into the low-mid 80s \nand dew points have dropped into the mid 50s in a few places. \nEfficient boundary layer mixing has allowed dew points to fall below \nguidance (as low as the 5th percentile). Additionally, high \ntemperatures may run a few degrees above guidance. Still, near \naverage readings are expected this afternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure along with light and variable winds may allow \nfor patchy fog tonight like previous nights. However, increasing \nhigh cloud cover tonight may limit coverage in comparison. Fog is \nmost probable along rivers and in rural/agricultural areas.\n\nAloft, troughing over the northeastern US will promote northwesterly \nflow across the Midwest. Ensemble guidance is in good agreement \nshowing a shortwave trough diving southeast within this flow, \narriving later Friday evening. Showers and thunderstorms are \npossible as early as Friday night. Guidance then slows the shortwave \ndown as western ridging amplifies. This should bring higher rain \nprobabilities into the day Saturday, potentially into early Sunday. \nLocally heavy rainfall amounts are possible owing to the slow system \nmotion.\n\nLooking into next week, the weekend shortwave departs eastward while \nthe polar jet lifts north. Though there is no strong signal for \neither troughing or ridging, the jet stream residing well to our \nnorth should allow temperatures to creep upwards. A warming trend is \ntherefore favored next week with highs potentially reaching into the \nupper 80s / near 90 at times. Some guidance keeps the jet stream \nactive, with frequent storm systems embedded within the broader \nflow. This may act to keep daily precipitation chances above average \nthrough next week as well. However, the signal regarding \nprecipitation is less clear than for temperatures.",
